1.2.1 The Concept of Being
The concept û is common to and foundational for all things, be-
cause ¡ understands ê to have the question “What isbeing?” at its core: “And indeed the question which was raised of old
and is raised now and always and is always the subject of doubt, ‘What
is being?’” ( Met. VII. 1028b2–4). The deliberate combination of “of old”
(palai), “now”  (nyn),  and “always”  (aei)  indicates that ¡ ể
ranks himself as a philosopher û ¤ tradition of Parmenidesand Plato, but that he considers ê’s central concern with being(“ấ being?” ti to on ) as holding true for all time. The “always” goes
õ assertion of unchangeable core importance far beyond Homer’sformula, which combines past, present, and future tense (en, estin, estai).
While ¡ realizes Ü earliest philosophers were concerned
more with becoming than with being, he ć interpret this as mean-
ing that ê of becoming is an alternative to ê Đbut rather that early ê ï forgiven for its as yet inad-equate grasp æ subject: “For the earliest ê is, on all subjects,
like one who lisps, since it is young and õ beginnings” ( Met. I. 993a15–
16). ¡ explicitly restates that being is a truly pervasive ẩ
constant, e.g., “and being is common to all things” ( koinon de pasi to on
estin ;Met. IV . 1004b20). The “common” (koinon)  echoes Heraclitus’s ear-
