Kranz, Frg. 2, lns. 2–3) and, like it, is unrestricted õ generality. Being
pertains to all things without exception (pasi). Its equally unrestricted
foundational importance is reflected in ¡’s technical vocabulary,
which designates all things as beings (onta),  collectively as all beings
(panta ta onta)  źdividually as a being (on). From an earlier colloquial
meaning as things in general and as property or possessions in particu-lar, beings (onta)  was elevated § role £ core technical philosophi-
cal concept by Parmenides (¤ singular to on ) and by Plato (¤
singular and plural to on and ta onta ). It is so retained by ¡. Philo-
